KNOWNBUG: std::string character data is not initialised on construction.

libstdc++ provides std::__cxx11::basic_string<char> as an explicit
instantiation declaration (extern template).  CBMC realises that class via
the class_template_symbol / elaborate_class_template / parser-completion
path, which (unlike instantiate_template) never runs the
deferred-method-conversion loop for the instance's inline members.  The
character-copy helpers basic_string<char>::_S_copy_chars -> _S_copy keep an
un-converted body in deferred_typechecking and are then discarded (made nil)
by cpp_typecheckt::clean_up(), so the copy becomes a no-op: size() is correct
but the characters stay non-deterministic.

Grounded in N5008 [temp.inst]/4 and [temp.inst] Note 4 (an inline member that
is the subject of an explicit instantiation declaration must still be
implicitly instantiated when odr-used).  See
doc/architectural/cpp-extern-template-member-instantiation.md.

When the extern-template member-instantiation fix lands, the two character
assertions become SUCCESS and verification succeeds; reclassify to CORE.
